$\delta$ maps $(\mathfrak{A}^{(m)}:\mathfrak{P})$ linearly into $(\mathfrak{A}^{(m+1)}:\mathfrak{P})$. If
$f\in(\mathfrak{A}^{(m)}:\mathfrak{P})$,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta f\{a_1,\cdots,a_{m+1}\}
&=a_1\cdot f\{a_2,\cdots,a_{m+1}\}\\
&\quad+\sum_{i=1}^{m}(-1)^i f\{a_1,\cdots,a_i a_{i+1},\cdots,a_{m+1}\}\\
&\quad+(-1)^{m+1}f\{a_1,\cdots,a_m\}\cdot a_{m+1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Let $\{\mathfrak{B},\sigma\}$ be a singular extension of $\mathfrak{A}$ with kernel $\mathfrak{K}$. Let $\rho$ be any linear mapping of $\mathfrak{A}$ into $\mathfrak{B}$ such that
\[
\sigma\{\rho\{a\}\}=a,\quad\hbox{for every } a\in\mathfrak{A},\quad\hbox{or---which is equivalent---}
\]
\begin{equation}\tag{1}
\rho\{\sigma\{b\}\}\equiv b\bmod\mathfrak{K},\quad\hbox{for every }b\in\mathfrak{B}.
\end{equation}

We can make $\mathfrak{K}$ a two-sided $\mathfrak{A}$-module by the definitions $a\cdot k=\rho\{a\}k$, $k\cdot a=k\rho\{a\}$, where $k\in\mathfrak{K}$, $a\in\mathfrak{A}$.

$\delta$ maps $(\mathfrak{A}^{(m)}:\mathfrak{P})$ linearly into $(\mathfrak{A}^{(m+1)}:\mathfrak{P})$. If
$f\in(\mathfrak{A}^{(m)}:\mathfrak{P})$,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta f\{a_1,\cdots,a_{m+1}\}
&=a_1\cdot f\{a_2,\cdots,a_{m+1}\}\\
&\quad+\sum_{i=1}^{m}(-1)^i f\{a_1,\cdots,a_i a_{i+1},\cdots,a_{m+1}\}\\
&\quad+(-1)^{m+1}f\{a_1,\cdots,a_m\}\cdot a_{m+1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

For $m\geqq 1$, the $m$-dimensional cohomology group of $\mathfrak{A}$ for the module
$\mathfrak{P}$, denoted by $H^m(\mathfrak{A},\mathfrak{P})$, is the group of the $(m,\mathfrak{P})$-cocycles
of $\mathfrak{A}$, modulo the subgroup of coboundaries.\textsuperscript{6}

